## sn22.56
sn22.56:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī. There the Blessed One said,
sn22.56:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five grasping aggregates. | T: “Monks, there are these five clinging-aggregates.
sn22.56:1.3 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn22.56:1.4 | S: The grasping aggregates of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: The form clinging-aggregate, the feeling clinging-aggregate, the perception clinging-aggregate, the fabrications clinging-aggregate, the consciousness clinging-aggregate.
sn22.56:1.5 | S: As long as I didn’t truly understand these five grasping aggregates in four rounds, I didn’t announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: “Now, as long as I did not have direct knowledge of the fourfold round with regard to these five clinging-aggregates as they have come to be, I did not claim to have directly awakened to the unexcelled right self-awakening in this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & common folk.
sn22.56:1.6 | S: But when I did truly understand these five grasping aggregates in four rounds, I announced my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: But when I did have direct knowledge of the fourfold round with regard to these five clinging-aggregates as they have come to be, then I did claim to have directly awakened to the unexcelled right self-awakening in this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & common folk.
sn22.56:1.7 | P: sadevamanussāya anuttaraṁ sammāsambodhiṁ abhisambuddhoti paccaññāsiṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.56:2.1 | S: And how are there four rounds? | T: “The fourfold round in what way?
sn22.56:2.2 | S: I directly knew form, its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation. | T: I had direct knowledge of form… of the origination of form… of the cessation of form… of the path of practice leading to the cessation of form.
sn22.56:2.3 | S: I directly knew feeling … | T: “I had direct knowledge of feeling.…
sn22.56:2.4 | S: perception … | T: “I had direct knowledge of perception.…
sn22.56:2.5 | S: choices … | T: “I had direct knowledge of fabrications.…
sn22.56:2.6 | S: consciousness, its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation. | T: “I had direct knowledge of consciousness… of the origination of consciousness… of the cessation of consciousness… of the path of practice leading to the cessation of consciousness.
sn22.56:3.1 | S: And what is form? | T: “And what is form?
sn22.56:3.2 | S: The four principal states, and form derived from the four principal states. | T: The four great existents [the earth property, the liquid property, the fire property, & the wind property] and the form derived from them:
sn22.56:3.3 | S: This is called form. | T: This is called form.
sn22.56:3.4 | S: Form originates from fuel. | T: From the origination of nutriment comes the origination of form.
sn22.56:3.5 | S: When fuel ceases, form ceases. | T: From the cessation of nutriment comes the cessation of form.
sn22.56:3.6 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of form is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: And just this noble eightfold path is the path of practice leading to the cessation of form, i.e.,
sn22.56:3.7 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn22.56:4.1 | S: Whatever ascetics and brahmins have directly known form in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation—and are practicing for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding form: they are practicing well. | T: “For any contemplatives or brahmans who by directly knowing form in this way, directly knowing the origination of form in this way, directly knowing the cessation of form in this way, directly knowing the path of practice leading to the cessation of form in this way, are practicing for disenchantment—dispassion—cessation with regard to form, they are practicing rightly.
sn22.56:4.2 | S: Those who practice well have a footing in this teaching and training. | T: Those who are practicing rightly are firmly based in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
sn22.56:5.1 | S: Those ascetics and brahmins who have directly known form in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation—and due to disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding form, are freed by not grasping: they are well freed. | T: And any contemplatives or brahmans who by directly knowing form in this way, directly knowing the origination of form in this way, directly knowing the cessation of form in this way, directly knowing the path of practice leading to the cessation of form in this way, are—from disenchantment, dispassion, cessation, lack of clinging/sustenance with regard to form—released, they are well released.
sn22.56:5.2 | S: Those who are well freed are consummate ones. | T: Those who are well released are fully accomplished.
sn22.56:5.3 | S: For consummate ones, there is no cycle of rebirths to be found. | T: And with those who are fully accomplished, there is no cycle for the sake of describing them.
sn22.56:6.1 | S: And what is feeling? | T: “And what is feeling?
sn22.56:6.2 | S: There are these six classes of feeling: | T: These six bodies of feeling—
sn22.56:6.3 | S: feeling born of contact through the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind. | T: feeling born of eye-contact, feeling born of ear-contact, feeling born of nose-contact, feeling born of tongue-contact, feeling born of body-contact, feeling born of intellect-contact:
sn22.56:6.4 | S: This is called feeling. | T: This is called feeling.
sn22.56:6.5 | S: Feeling originates from contact. | T: From the origination of contact comes the origination of feeling.
sn22.56:6.6 | S: When contact ceases, feeling ceases. | T: From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling.
sn22.56:6.7 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of feelings is simply this noble eightfold path … | T: And just this noble eightfold path is the path of practice leading to the cessation of feeling.…
sn22.56:6.8 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: (none)
sn22.56:7.1 | P: Ye hi keci, bhikkhave, samaṇā vā brāhmaṇā vā evaṁ vedanaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ vedanāsamudayaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ vedanānirodhaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ vedanānirodhagāminiṁ paṭipadaṁ abhiññāya vedanāya nibbidāya virāgāya nirodhāya paṭipannā, te suppaṭipannā. | T: (none)
sn22.56:7.2 | P: Ye suppaṭipannā, te imasmiṁ dhammavinaye gādhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.56:8.1 | P: Ye ca kho keci, bhikkhave, samaṇā vā brāhmaṇā vā evaṁ vedanaṁ abhiññāya …pe… evaṁ vedanānirodhagāminiṁ paṭipadaṁ abhiññāya …pe… | T: (none)
sn22.56:8.2 | P: vaṭṭaṁ tesaṁ natthi paññāpanāya. | T: (none)
sn22.56:9.1 | S: And what is perception? | T: “And what is perception?
sn22.56:9.2 | S: There are these six classes of perception: | T: These six bodies of perception—
sn22.56:9.3 | S: perceptions of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and ideas. | T: perception of form, perception of sound, perception of smell, perception of taste, perception of tactile sensation, perception of ideas:
sn22.56:9.4 | S: This is called perception. | T: This is called perception.
sn22.56:9.5 | S: Perception originates from contact. | T: From the origination of contact comes the origination of perception.
sn22.56:9.6 | S: When contact ceases, perception ceases. | T: From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of perception.
sn22.56:9.7 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of perceptions is simply this noble eightfold path … | T: And just this noble eightfold path is the path of practice leading to the cessation of perception.…
sn22.56:9.8 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi …pe… | T: (none)
sn22.56:9.9 | P: vaṭṭaṁ tesaṁ natthi paññāpanāya. | T: (none)
sn22.56:10.1 | S: And what are choices? | T: “And what are fabrications?
sn22.56:10.2 | S: There are these six classes of intention: | T: These six bodies of intention—
sn22.56:10.3 | S: intention regarding sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and ideas. | T: intention with regard to form, intention with regard to sound, intention with regard to smell, intention with regard to taste, intention with regard to tactile sensation, intention with regard to ideas:
sn22.56:10.4 | S: These are called choices. | T: These are called fabrications.
sn22.56:10.5 | S: Choices originate from contact. | T: From the origination of contact comes the origination of fabrications.
sn22.56:10.6 | S: When contact ceases, choices cease. | T: From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of fabrications.
sn22.56:10.7 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of choices is simply this noble eightfold path … | T: And just this noble eightfold path is the path of practice leading to the cessation of fabrications.…
sn22.56:10.8 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: (none)
sn22.56:11.1 | P: Ye hi keci, bhikkhave, samaṇā vā brāhmaṇā vā evaṁ saṅkhāre abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhārasamudayaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhāranirodhaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhāranirodhagāminiṁ paṭipadaṁ abhiññāya saṅkhārānaṁ nibbidāya virāgāya nirodhāya paṭipannā, te suppaṭipannā. | T: (none)
sn22.56:11.2 | P: Ye suppaṭipannā, te imasmiṁ dhammavinaye gādhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.56:12.1 | P: Ye ca kho keci, bhikkhave, samaṇā vā brāhmaṇā vā evaṁ saṅkhāre abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhārasamudayaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhāranirodhaṁ abhiññāya, evaṁ saṅkhāranirodhagāminiṁ paṭipadaṁ abhiññāya saṅkhārānaṁ nibbidā virāgā nirodhā anupādā vimuttā, te suvimuttā. | T: (none)
sn22.56:12.2 | P: Ye suvimuttā, te kevalino. | T: (none)
sn22.56:12.3 | P: Ye kevalino vaṭṭaṁ tesaṁ natthi paññāpanāya. | T: (none)
sn22.56:13.1 | S: And what is consciousness? | T: “And what is consciousness?
sn22.56:13.2 | S: There are these six classes of consciousness: | T: These six bodies of consciousness—
sn22.56:13.3 | S: eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind consciousness. | T: eye-consciousness, ear-consciousness, nose-consciousness, tongue-consciousness, body-consciousness, intellect-consciousness:
sn22.56:13.4 | S: This is called consciousness. | T: This is called consciousness.
sn22.56:13.5 | S: Consciousness originates from name and form. | T: From the origination of name-&-form comes the origination of consciousness.
sn22.56:13.6 | S: When name and form cease, consciousness ceases. | T: From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of consciousness.
sn22.56:13.7 | S: The practice that leads to the cessation of consciousness is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: And just this noble eightfold path is the path of practice leading to the cessation of consciousness, i.e.,
sn22.56:13.8 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn22.56:14.1 | S: Whatever ascetics and brahmins have directly known consciousness in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation—and are practicing for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding consciousness: they are practicing well. | T: “For any contemplatives or brahmans who by directly knowing consciousness in this way, directly knowing the origination of consciousness in this way, directly knowing the cessation of consciousness in this way, directly knowing the path of practice leading to the cessation of consciousness in this way, are practicing for disenchantment—dispassion—cessation with regard to consciousness, they are practicing rightly.
sn22.56:14.2 | S: Those who practice well have a footing in this teaching and training. | T: Those who are practicing rightly are firmly based in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
sn22.56:15.1 | S: Those ascetics and brahmins who have directly known consciousness in this way—and its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation—and due to disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding consciousness, are freed by not grasping: they are well freed. | T: And any contemplatives or brahmans who by directly knowing consciousness in this way, directly knowing the origination of consciousness in this way, directly knowing the cessation of consciousness in this way, directly knowing the path of practice leading to the cessation of consciousness in this way, are—from disenchantment, dispassion, cessation, lack of clinging/sustenance with regard to consciousness—released, they are well released.
sn22.56:15.2 | S: Those who are well freed are consummate ones. | T: Those who are well released are fully accomplished.
sn22.56:15.3 | S: For consummate ones, there is no cycle of rebirths to be found.” | T: And with those who are fully accomplished, there is no cycle for the sake of describing them.”
sn22.56:15.4 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

