## sn22.5
sn22.5:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn22.5:1.2 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn22.5:1.3 | P: tatra kho …pe… etadavoca: | T: There he addressed the monks: “Monks!” “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him. The Blessed One said:
sn22.5:1.4 | S: “Mendicants, develop immersion. | T: “Develop concentration, monks.
sn22.5:1.5 | S: A mendicant who has immersion truly understands. | T: A concentrated monk discerns in line with what has come into being.
sn22.5:1.6 | S: What do they truly understand? | T: And what does he discern in line with what has come into being?
sn22.5:1.7 | S: The origin and ending of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: The origination & disappearance of form. The origination & disappearance of feeling… perception… fabrications. The origination & disappearance of consciousness.
sn22.5:2.1 | S: And what is the origin of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness? | T: “And what is the origination of form… feeling… perception… fabrications? What is the origination of consciousness?
sn22.5:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging. | T: “There is the case where one enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened.
sn22.5:3.1 | S: What do they approve, welcome, and keep clinging to? | T: And what does one enjoy & welcome, to what does one remain fastened?
sn22.5:3.2 | S: They approve, welcome, and keep clinging to form. | T: One enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened to form. As one enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened to form,
sn22.5:3.3 | S: This gives rise to relishing. | T: there arises delight.
sn22.5:3.4 | S: Relishing forms is grasping. | T: Any delight in form is clinging.
sn22.5:3.5 | S: Their grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
sn22.5:3.6 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
sn22.5:3.7 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn22.5:3.8 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn22.5:4.1 | S: They approve, welcome, and keep clinging to feeling … | T: “One enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened to feeling…
sn22.5:4.2 | S: perception … | T: perception…
sn22.5:4.3 | S: choices … | T: fabrications…
sn22.5:4.4 | S: consciousness. | T: “One enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened to consciousness.
sn22.5:4.5 | S: This gives rise to relishing. | T: As one enjoys, welcomes, & remains fastened to consciousness, there arises delight.
sn22.5:4.6 | S: Relishing consciousness is grasping. | T: Any delight in consciousness is clinging.
sn22.5:4.7 | S: Their grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
sn22.5:4.8 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
sn22.5:4.9 | S: Rebirth is a condition that gives rise to old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
sn22.5:4.10 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn22.5:5.1 | S: This is the origin of form, | T: “This, monks, is the origination of form.
sn22.5:5.2 | S: feeling, | T: This, the origination of feeling…
sn22.5:5.3 | S: perception, | T: perception…
sn22.5:5.4 | S: choices, | T: fabrications.
sn22.5:5.5 | S: and consciousness. | T: This, the origination of consciousness.”
sn22.5:6.1 | S: And what is the ending of form, feeling, | T: “And what is the disappearance of form… feeling…
sn22.5:6.2 | S: perception, | T: perception…
sn22.5:6.3 | S: choices, | T: fabrications?
sn22.5:6.4 | S: and consciousness? | T: What is the disappearance of consciousness?
sn22.5:7.1 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, or keep clinging. | T: “There is the case where one doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened.
sn22.5:8.1 | S: What don’t they approve, welcome, or keep clinging to? | T: And what does one not enjoy or welcome, to what does one not remain fastened?
sn22.5:8.2 | S: They don’t approve, welcome, or keep clinging to form. | T: One doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened to form. As one doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened to form,
sn22.5:8.3 | S: As a result, relishing of form ceases. | T: any delight in form ceases.
sn22.5:8.4 | S: When that relishing ceases, grasping ceases. | T: From the cessation of delight comes the cessation of clinging.
sn22.5:8.5 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. … | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance, the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming, the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn22.5:8.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn22.5:9.1 | S: They don’t approve, welcome, or keep clinging to feeling … | T: “One doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened to feeling…
sn22.5:9.2 | P: Tassa vedanaṁ anabhinandato anabhivadato anajjhosāya tiṭṭhato yā vedanāya nandī sā nirujjhati. | T: (none)
sn22.5:9.3 | P: Tassa nandīnirodhā upādānanirodho; | T: (none)
sn22.5:9.4 | P: upādānanirodhā bhavanirodho …pe… | T: (none)
sn22.5:9.5 | P: evametassa kevalassa dukkhakkhandhassa nirodho hoti. | T: (none)
