sn22.5:10.2 | S: choices … | T: fabrications…
sn22.5:10.3 | P: Tassa saṅkhāre anabhinandato anabhivadato anajjhosāya tiṭṭhato yā saṅkhāresu nandī sā nirujjhati. | T: (none)
sn22.5:10.4 | P: Tassa nandīnirodhā upādānanirodho; | T: (none)
sn22.5:10.5 | P: upādānanirodhā bhavanirodho …pe… | T: (none)
sn22.5:10.6 | P: evametassa kevalassa dukkhakkhandhassa nirodho hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.5:11.1 | S: consciousness. | T: “One doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened to consciousness.
sn22.5:11.2 | S: As a result, relishing of consciousness ceases. | T: As one doesn’t enjoy, welcome, or remain fastened to consciousness, any delight in consciousness ceases.
sn22.5:11.3 | S: When that relishing ceases, grasping ceases. … | T: From the cessation of delight comes the cessation of clinging. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance, the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming, the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
sn22.5:11.4 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
sn22.5:12.1 | S: This is the ending of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness.” | T: “This, monks, is the disappearance of form. This, the disappearance of feeling… perception… fabrications. This, the disappearance of consciousness.”
sn22.5:12.2 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.60
sn22.60:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn22.60:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesāli at the Gabled Hall in the Great Forest.
sn22.60:1.3 | S: Then Mahāli the Licchavi went up to the Buddha … and said to him: | T: Then Mahāli the Licchavi went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn22.60:2.1 | S: “Sir, Pūraṇa Kassapa says this: | T: “Pūraṇa Kassapa says this:
sn22.60:2.2 | S: ‘There is no cause or reason for the corruption of sentient beings. | T: ‘There is no cause, no requisite condition, for the defilement of beings.
sn22.60:2.3 | S: Sentient beings are corrupted without cause or reason. | T: Beings are defiled without cause, without requisite condition.
sn22.60:2.4 | S: There’s no cause or reason for the purification of sentient beings. | T: There is no cause, no requisite condition, for the purification of beings.
sn22.60:2.5 | S: Sentient beings are purified without cause or reason.’ | T: Beings are purified without cause, without requisite condition.’
sn22.60:2.6 | S: What does the Buddha say about this?” | T: What does the Blessed One say about this?”
sn22.60:3.1 | S: “Mahāli, there is a cause and reason for the corruption of sentient beings. | T: “Mahāli, there is cause, there is requisite condition, for the defilement of beings.
sn22.60:3.2 | S: Sentient beings are corrupted with cause and reason. | T: Beings are defiled with cause, with requisite condition.
sn22.60:3.3 | S: There is a cause and reason for the purification of sentient beings. | T: There is cause, there is requisite condition, for the purification of beings.
sn22.60:3.4 | S: Sentient beings are purified with cause and reason.” | T: Beings are purified with cause, with requisite condition.”
sn22.60:4.1 | S: “But sir, what is the cause and reason for the corruption of sentient beings? | T: “And what, lord, is the cause, what the requisite condition, for the defilement of beings?
sn22.60:4.2 | S: How are sentient beings corrupted with cause and reason?” | T: How are beings defiled with cause, with requisite condition?”
sn22.60:5.1 | S: “Mahāli, if form were absolutely painful—soaked and steeped in pain and not steeped in pleasure—sentient beings wouldn’t be aroused by it. | T: “Mahāli, if form were exclusively stressful—followed by stress, infused with stress and not infused with pleasure—beings would not be infatuated with form.
sn22.60:5.2 | S: But because form is pleasurable—soaked and steeped in pleasure and not steeped in pain—sentient beings are aroused by it. | T: But because form is also pleasurable—followed by pleasure, infused with pleasure and not infused with stress—beings are infatuated with form.
sn22.60:5.3 | S: Since they are aroused by it, they’re caught up in it, and so they become corrupted. | T: Through infatuation, they are captivated. Through captivation, they are defiled.
sn22.60:5.4 | S: This is a cause and reason for the corruption of sentient beings. | T: This is the cause, this the requisite condition, for the defilement of beings.
sn22.60:5.5 | S: This is how sentient beings are corrupted with cause and reason. | T: And this is how beings are defiled with cause, with requisite condition.
sn22.60:6.1 | S: If feeling … | T: “If feeling were exclusively stressful.…
sn22.60:6.2 | P: Yasmā ca kho, mahāli, vedanā sukhā sukhānupatitā sukhāvakkantā anavakkantā dukkhena, tasmā sattā vedanāya sārajjanti; | T: (none)
sn22.60:6.3 | P: sārāgā saṁyujjanti; | T: (none)
sn22.60:6.4 | P: saṁyogā saṅkilissanti. | T: (none)
sn22.60:6.5 | P: Ayampi kho, mahāli, hetu, ayaṁ paccayo sattānaṁ saṅkilesāya. | T: (none)
sn22.60:6.6 | P: Evampi sahetū sappaccayā sattā saṅkilissanti. | T: (none)
sn22.60:7.1 | S: perception … | T: “If perception were exclusively stressful.…
sn22.60:7.2 | S: choices … | T: “If fabrications were exclusively stressful.…
sn22.60:7.3 | P: Yasmā ca kho, mahāli, saṅkhārā sukhā sukhānupatitā sukhāvakkantā anavakkantā dukkhena, tasmā sattā saṅkhāresu sārajjanti; | T: (none)
sn22.60:7.4 | P: sārāgā saṁyujjanti; | T: (none)
sn22.60:7.5 | P: saṁyogā saṅkilissanti. | T: (none)
sn22.60:7.6 | P: Ayampi kho, mahāli, hetu, ayaṁ paccayo sattānaṁ saṅkilesāya. | T: (none)
sn22.60:7.7 | P: Evampi sahetū sappaccayā sattā saṅkilissanti. | T: (none)
sn22.60:8.1 | S: consciousness were absolutely painful—soaked and steeped in pain and not steeped in pleasure—sentient beings wouldn’t be aroused by it. | T: “If consciousness were exclusively stressful—followed by stress, infused with stress and not infused with pleasure—beings would not be infatuated with consciousness.
sn22.60:8.2 | S: But because consciousness is pleasurable—soaked and steeped in pleasure and not steeped in pain—sentient beings are aroused by it. | T: But because consciousness is also pleasurable—followed by pleasure, infused with pleasure and not infused with stress—beings are infatuated with consciousness.
sn22.60:8.3 | S: Since they are aroused by it, they’re caught up in it, and so they become corrupted. | T: Through infatuation, they are captivated. Through captivation, they are defiled.
sn22.60:8.4 | S: This is a cause and reason for the corruption of sentient beings. | T: This is the cause, this the requisite condition, for the defilement of beings.
sn22.60:8.5 | S: This is how sentient beings are corrupted with cause and reason.” | T: And this is how beings are defiled with cause, with requisite condition.”
sn22.60:9.1 | S: “But sir, what is the cause and reason for the purification of sentient beings? | T: “And what, lord, is the cause, what the requisite condition, for the purification of beings?
sn22.60:9.2 | S: How are sentient beings purified with cause and reason?” | T: How are beings purified with cause, with requisite condition?”
sn22.60:9.3 | S: “Mahāli, if form was absolutely pleasurable—soaked and steeped in pleasure and not steeped in pain—sentient beings wouldn’t grow disillusioned with it. | T: “Mahāli, if form were exclusively pleasurable—followed by pleasure, infused with pleasure and not infused with stress—beings would not be disenchanted with form.
sn22.60:9.4 | S: But because form is painful—soaked and steeped in pain and not steeped in pleasure—sentient beings do grow disillusioned with it. | T: But because form is also stressful—followed by stress, infused with stress and not infused with pleasure—beings are disenchanted with form.
sn22.60:9.5 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they are purified. | T: Disenchanted, they become dispassionate. Through dispassion, they are purified.
sn22.60:9.6 | S: This is a cause and reason for the purification of sentient beings. | T: This is the cause, this the requisite condition, for the purification of beings.
sn22.60:9.7 | S: This is how sentient beings are purified with cause and reason. | T: And this is how beings are purified with cause, with requisite condition.
sn22.60:10.1 | S: If feeling … | T: “If feeling were exclusively pleasurable.…
sn22.60:10.2 | S: perception … | T: “If perception were exclusively pleasurable.…
sn22.60:10.3 | S: choices … | T: “If fabrications were exclusively pleasurable.…
sn22.60:10.4 | S: consciousness was absolutely pleasurable—soaked and steeped in pleasure and not steeped in pain—sentient beings wouldn’t grow disillusioned with it. | T: “If consciousness were exclusively pleasurable—followed by pleasure, infused with pleasure and not infused with stress—beings would not be disenchanted with consciousness.
sn22.60:10.5 | S: But because consciousness is painful—soaked and steeped in pain and not steeped in pleasure—sentient beings do grow disillusioned with it. | T: But because consciousness is also stressful—followed by stress, infused with stress and not infused with pleasure—beings are disenchanted with consciousness.
sn22.60:10.6 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they are purified. | T: Disenchanted, they become dispassionate. Through dispassion, they are purified.
sn22.60:10.7 | S: This is a cause and reason for the purification of sentient beings. | T: This is the cause, this the requisite condition, for the purification of beings.
sn22.60:10.8 | S: This is how sentient beings are purified with cause and reason.” | T: And this is how beings are purified with cause, with requisite condition.”
sn22.60:10.9 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.79
sn22.79:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn22.79:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, whatever ascetics and brahmins recollect many kinds of past lives, all recollect the five grasping aggregates, or one of them. | T: “Monks, any contemplatives or brahmans who recollect their manifold past lives all recollect the five clinging-aggregates, or one among them.
sn22.79:1.3 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn22.79:1.4 | S: ‘I had such form in the past.’ | T: When recollecting, ‘I was one with such a form in the past,’
sn22.79:1.5 | S: Recollecting thus, it’s only form that they recollect. | T: one is recollecting just form.
sn22.79:1.6 | S: ‘I had such feeling … | T: Or when recollecting, ‘I was one with such a feeling in the past,’
sn22.79:1.7 | P: iti vā hi, bhikkhave, anussaramāno vedanaṁyeva anussarati. | T: one is recollecting just feeling.
sn22.79:1.8 | S: perception … | T: Or when recollecting, ‘I was one with such a perception in the past,’ one is recollecting just perception.
sn22.79:1.9 | S: choices … | T: Or when recollecting, ‘I was one with such fabrications in the past,’ one is recollecting just fabrications.
sn22.79:1.10 | S: consciousness in the past.’ | T: Or when recollecting, ‘I was one with such a consciousness in the past,’
sn22.79:1.11 | S: Recollecting thus, it’s only consciousness that they recollect. | T: one is recollecting just consciousness.
sn22.79:2.1 | S: And why do you call it form? | T: “And why do you call it ‘form’ [rūpa]?
sn22.79:2.2 | S: It’s deformed; that’s why it’s called ‘form’. | T: ‘It is afflicted [ruppati],’ thus it is called ‘form.’
sn22.79:2.3 | S: Deformed by what? | T: Afflicted with what?
sn22.79:2.4 | S: Deformed by cold, heat, hunger, and thirst, and deformed by the touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, and reptiles. | T: With cold & heat & hunger & thirst, with the touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, & reptiles.
sn22.79:2.5 | S: It’s deformed; that’s why it’s called ‘form’. | T: ‘It is afflicted,’ thus it is called ‘form.’
sn22.79:3.1 | S: And why do you call it feeling? | T: “And why do you call it ‘feeling’?
sn22.79:3.2 | S: It feels; that’s why it’s called ‘feeling’. | T: ‘It feels,’ thus it is called ‘feeling.’
sn22.79:3.3 | S: And what does it feel? | T: What does it feel?
sn22.79:3.4 | S: It feels pleasure, pain, and neutral. | T: It feels pleasure, it feels pain, it feels neither-pleasure-nor-pain.
sn22.79:3.5 | S: It feels; that’s why it’s called ‘feeling’. | T: ‘It feels,’ thus it is called ‘feeling.’
sn22.79:4.1 | S: And why do you call it perception? | T: “And why do you call it ‘perception’?
sn22.79:4.2 | S: It perceives; that’s why it’s called ‘perception’. | T: ‘It perceives,’ thus it is called ‘perception.’
sn22.79:4.3 | S: And what does it perceive? | T: What does it perceive?
sn22.79:4.4 | S: It perceives blue, yellow, red, and white. | T: It perceives blue, it perceives yellow, it perceives red, & it perceives white.
sn22.79:4.5 | S: It perceives; that’s why it’s called ‘perception’. | T: ‘It perceives,’ thus it is called ‘perception.’
sn22.79:5.1 | S: And why do you call them choices? | T: “And why do you call them ‘fabrications’?
sn22.79:5.2 | S: Choices produce conditioned phenomena; that’s why they’re called ‘choices’. | T: ‘They fabricate the fabricated,’ thus they are called ‘fabrications.’
sn22.79:5.3 | S: And what are the conditioned phenomena that they produce? | T: And what is the fabricated that they fabricate?
sn22.79:5.4 | S: Form is a conditioned phenomenon; choices are what make it into form. Feeling is a conditioned phenomenon; choices are what make it into feeling. Perception is a conditioned phenomenon; choices are what make it into perception. Choices are conditioned phenomena; choices are what make them into choices. Consciousness is a conditioned phenomenon; choices are what make it into consciousness. | T: For the sake of form-ness, they fabricate fabricated form. For the sake of feeling-ness, they fabricate fabricated feeling. For the sake of perception-hood… For the sake of fabrication-hood… For the sake of consciousness-hood, they fabricate fabricated consciousness.
sn22.79:5.5 | S: Choices produce conditioned phenomena; that’s why they’re called ‘choices’. | T: ‘They fabricate the fabricated,’ thus they are called ‘fabrications.’
sn22.79:6.1 | S: And why do you call it consciousness? | T: “And why do you call it ‘consciousness’?
sn22.79:6.2 | S: It cognizes; that’s why it’s called ‘consciousness’. | T: ‘It cognizes,’ thus it is called ‘consciousness.’
sn22.79:6.3 | S: And what does it cognize? | T: What does it cognize?
sn22.79:6.4 | S: It cognizes sour, bitter, pungent, sweet, hot, mild, salty, and bland. | T: It cognizes sour, it cognizes bitter, it cognizes pungent, it cognizes sweet, it cognizes alkaline, it cognizes non-alkaline, it cognizes salty, & it cognizes unsalty.
sn22.79:6.5 | S: It cognizes; that’s why it’s called ‘consciousness’. | T: ‘It cognizes,’ thus it is called ‘consciousness.’
sn22.79:7.1 | S: A learned noble disciple reflects on this: | T: “Thus an instructed disciple of the noble ones reflects in this way:
sn22.79:7.2 | S: ‘Currently I’m bitten by form. | T: ‘I am now being chewed up by form.
sn22.79:7.3 | S: In the past I was also bitten by form just like now. | T: But in the past I was also chewed up by form in the same way I am now being chewed up by present form.
sn22.79:7.4 | S: If I were to look forward to enjoying form in the future, I’d be bitten by form in the future just as I am today.’ | T: And if I delight in future form, then in the future I will be chewed up by form in the same way I am now being chewed up by present form.’
sn22.79:7.5 | S: Reflecting like this they are not concerned with past form, | T: Having reflected in this way, he becomes indifferent to past form,
sn22.79:7.6 | S: they don’t look forward to enjoying future form, | T: does not delight in future form,
sn22.79:7.7 | S: and they practice for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding present form. | T: and is practicing for the sake of disenchantment, dispassion, and cessation with regard to present form.
sn22.79:8.1 | S: ‘Currently I’m bitten by feeling … | T: “(He reflects:) ‘I am now being chewed up by feeling…
